at a park and ride on the outskirts of  Salisbury soldiers are testing for kovat  19 this is the first of 96 Ministry of  Defense mobile units being deployed in  the coming weeks one of these units is  designed to carry enough test kits for  500 tests a day but by virtue of the  nature of a mobile testing unit it may  we'll have to move to a number of  different locations in one day so that  isn't specifically the target but it has  the capacity to carry that we would  expect realistically to get about 300  tests done in one day and we know that  we can deliver about 50 tests in one  hour quite comfortably these units can  be set up in as little as 20 minutes  those needing a test first check in with  a soldier at the entrance to this  carpark  they're then told to drive around you  can see there's a couple of tents at the  back that's where they pick up the tests  they're handed to them through the  driver's window if there's any problems  they're told to put their hazards on and  they'll come and help once the test is  done they drop it off to the soldiers at  the Gazebo over there as you can see  there's not much to it and that really  is the whole point these are mobile  Rapid Response units which can be  deployed to wherever testing is needed  the units will travel to frontline staff  in places like prisons police stations  in care homes it's welcome news for  staff at Grove Care in Bristol they were  told their nearest testing site was a  125 mile round trip to Worcester it is  so important for us as nursing homes to  be able to have quick easy access to  testing but also quick results so that  we know what we're doing a lot of us  staff team are carers and nurses go into  isolation if they've been running at  high temperature and often we find that  you know someone's been in isolation for  a couple of weeks has made it really  challenging for the team last week key  workers outside of the NHS will also put  on the priority list for testing the  government has reiterated its aim of  carrying out a hundred thousand tests  day by the end of April it's currently  around 29,000 with just four days to hit  that target it could be down to the army  to help make that happen  dan y said Sky News in Salisbury
 

